`context/instructions/authoring.md` is the contract for writing or editing an
instruction file, an agent skill, or an AGENTS.md rule. Read it before adding
guidance an agent will follow.

## A description is a trigger, not a summary

State **when to use** the guidance, not what it does. A description that
summarises the workflow becomes a shortcut the agent follows instead of reading
the body.

- Good: "Use when tests have race conditions or pass/fail inconsistently."
- Bad: "Use for flaky tests: wait for the condition, then assert."

Describe the problem (a symptom, a situation), keep it technology-agnostic
unless the guidance is technology-specific, and write in the third person.

## Spend the reader's context

Progressive disclosure: keep always-loaded text short, push detail into an
on-demand file.

- Word budget: a frequently-loaded instruction under ~200 words; any other under
  ~500. Check with `wc -w`.
- Point at a command's `--help` instead of restating every flag.
- Cross-reference by name with an explicit marker; never force-include a file
  with `@` — it loads immediately and burns context.
- Compress examples, and do not repeat what a referenced file already says.

## Author from a failure, not from a feeling

Watch an agent fail without the rule first (RED); write the rule that addresses
exactly that failure, then confirm compliance (GREEN). Wording never tested
against a failure is a guess.

Match the form to the failure (form-to-failure):

| Baseline failure | Right form |
|---|---|
| Knows the rule and skips it under pressure | Prohibition + rationalization table + red flags |
| Complies but the output has the wrong shape | Positive recipe: state the shape of the output |
| Omits a required element | A REQUIRED slot in the template being filled |
| Behaviour depends on a condition | A conditional keyed to an observable predicate |

Prohibitions backfire on shaping problems — prefer a recipe. Add no nuance
clauses ("... unless it matters" reopens the negotiation); an exemption clause
does not scope — restructure instead.

## Micro-test the wording

Before a full run, test the wording: one fresh context per sample, with the
guidance in its realistic surroundings; a **no-guidance control** every time (if
the control does not fail, there is nothing to fix); 5+ repetitions, every
flagged match read by hand. Variance is a signal — five interpretations mean the
wording is not binding.

## Carry a rule, do not graft it

A rule, template or commit carried from another project or reference names none
of its source; generalise it first, then cite the target's own files.

## Where a rule belongs

- Project conventions → the AGENTS.md project block.
- A judgement call the agent must make → an instruction file (here).
- A mechanical constraint enforceable by a check → automation (a test or lint),
  not prose.
- A new type in a closed register lands in every surface that enumerates it —
  register, vocabulary, generated row, template, fixture — in one change.

Prose editing — machine-generated patterns, the minimum effective edit,
edit-vs-detect mode — is `context/instructions/writing.md`.
